---
/**
 * The three source lists a concept page shows, in one place (#362).
 *
 * WRITTEN TWICE BEFORE THIS, ONCE PER TIER, and growing. The frontier and
 * verified branches of `[id].astro` each carried a byte-identical citation `ul`,
 * a ~23-line `origin` block and a ~30-line `explainers` block — about sixty
 * duplicated lines, and the count went from two lists to three when ADR-0017
 * landed. The next person to change how a source renders had two places to
 * remember, and the comment above the second copy had already decayed to a
 * pointer at this issue.
 *
 * WHAT DIFFERS BETWEEN THE TIERS STAYS IN THE PAGE: the section chrome, the
 * eyebrow, the "drafted, awaiting review" line, and whether `ReviewActions`
 * renders. Those are real differences. The sources are not one of them —
 * ADR-0002 made `citations` top-level and tier-independent precisely so a node
 * keeps its sources when it is verified, and rendering them from two places was
 * the one way that guarantee could quietly stop holding.
 */
import type { AuthoredNode } from '../lib/graph';

interface Props {
  node: AuthoredNode;
}
const { node } = Astro.props;
---

<ul role="list" class="mt-3 space-y-1.5">
  {node.citations.map((c) => (
    <li class="font-mono text-[11.5px] text-dust">
      <a href={c.url} class="text-thread no-underline hover:underline">{c.ref}</a> — {c.title}
    </li>
  ))}
</ul>
{node.origin && node.origin.length > 0 && (
  <>
    {/*
      ADR-0013. Rendered as TEXT, never as links, and under a label of
      its own — these are sources nothing fetched. Presenting an
      asserted attribution in the same violet as a checked one would
      tell the reader the two carry the same weight, which is the
      single outcome that ADR exists to prevent.
    */}
    <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-dust">
      Origin · not linkable
    </p>
    <ul role="list" class="mt-2 space-y-1.5">
      {node.origin.map((o) => (
        <li class="font-mono text-[11.5px] text-dust">
          {o.ref} — {o.title}
          {o.venue && <span> · {o.venue}</span>}
          {o.doi && <span> · doi:{o.doi}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </>
)}
{node.explainers && node.explainers.length > 0 && (
  <>
    {/*
      ADR-0017. A third source list with a third job: `citations` is
      what backs the claims, `Origin` is where the idea came from,
      and this is where to go and be taught it.

      Linked in `thread`, unlike origin, and that difference is
      earned rather than cosmetic: `check:explainers` fetches each
      one, and for a video compares the title and author here
      against what YouTube reports. These are the most-checked links
      on the page.
    */}
    <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[.16em] text-dust">
      Watch &amp; read
    </p>
    <ul role="list" class="mt-2 space-y-1.5">
      {node.explainers.map((e) => (
        <li class="font-mono text-[11.5px] text-dust">
          <a href={e.url} class="text-thread no-underline hover:underline">{e.title}</a> — {e.author}
          {/*
            MARKED IN TEXT, AND ONLY THE EXCEPTION IS MARKED (#389).
            The heading has said "Watch & read" since ADR-0017, but
            every entry was a `read`, so a video and a blog post
            rendered identically — a reader clicking to skim got a
            26-minute lecture instead. Not colour, not an icon
            alone: CLAUDE.md §4 keeps meaning off a single ambiguous
            carrier. `read` stays unmarked because it is the default
            and labelling 482 of them is noise.
          */}
          {e.kind === 'video' && <span> · video</span>}
          {/*
            ADR-0018. A domain-scoped entry is NOT about this concept,
            and the reader is told before they click rather than after.
            Same rule ADR-0013 applies to origin: a broader source
            presented as a narrower one is the misrepresentation.

            ADR-0021: which domain, when the entry names one. Absent
            means the primary, which is what every entry written before
            that ADR meant implicitly.
          */}
          {e.scope === 'domain' && <span> · on {e.domain ?? node.domain[0]}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </>
)}
